<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angkor Wat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> Wat is a vast temple complex at Angkor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, built in the twelfth century under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii">Suryavarman II</a> and originally dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. It later became a Buddhist site. It is the largest religious monument in the world and the best-known symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-wat.jpg" alt="Angkor Wat" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørn Christian Tørrissen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>War elephants served as instruments of battlefield dominance, providing both physical and psychological impact, while infantry fought with spears, swords and shields, as the reliefs of <b>Angkor Wat</b> and the Bayon vividly depict.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Angkor Wat</b> in Cambodia, often called the largest religious monument in the world, was built in the early 12th century as a temple to Vishnu.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Borobudur ranks with Bagan in Myanmar and <b>Angkor Wat</b> in Cambodia among the great archaeological sites of Southeast Asia, and in 2012 it was recorded in the Guinness Book of World Records as the world's largest Buddhist temple.</blockquote><a href="/work/borobudur">Borobudur · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…traders carried the gods across the Bay of Bengal, and from the early centuries CE kingdoms in Funan, Champa, Java and the Khmer lands adopted Sanskrit, Hindu rites and the worship of Shiva and Vishnu, often alongside Buddhism; <b>Angkor Wat</b> was built for Vishnu in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>At its head stood a king whom the cult of the devaraja, the “god-king”, identified with Shiva or Vishnu, or under Jayavarman VII (r. 1181–1219) with the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, and whose temples, <b>Angkor Wat</b> and the Bayon, celebrated his divine rule on earth.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
